<file path=".cursorrules">
# Prisha Tracker — Cursor Rules

## Project Overview
This is a React Native + Expo app for tracking ימי פרישה (Jewish family purity laws).
It supports iOS, Android, and Web from a single codebase.

## Tech Stack
- Framework: React Native + Expo (SDK 51+) with Expo Router (file-based routing)
- Backend: Firebase (Firestore, Auth, Cloud Functions)
- Auth: Google Sign-In, Apple Sign-In, Email/Password
- State: Zustand
- Styling: NativeWind (Tailwind for React Native)
- i18n: i18next + react-i18next (Hebrew + English, full RTL support for Hebrew)
- Hebrew calendar: @hebcal/core
- TypeScript: strict mode

## Project Structure
Follow the structure in docs/PLANNING.md exactly.
All halachic calculations live in src/calculations/ as pure functions with no UI or Firebase imports.
All Firebase logic lives in src/firebase/.
All screens live in app/.
All reusable components live in src/components/.

## Critical Rules
- ALWAYS use TypeScript. Never write plain .js files.
- ALWAYS write RTL-compatible layouts (use 'start'/'end' not 'left'/'right').
- NEVER put business logic inside screen files — screens are thin, logic goes in hooks or the calculations engine.
- ALWAYS add a JSDoc comment to every calculation function citing the halachic source.
- NEVER import Firebase inside src/calculations/ — that folder must stay pure.
- When creating a new component always ask: does this belong in src/components/common, calendar, period, or settings?
- All user-facing strings must go through i18n — never hardcode Hebrew or English text.

## Minhagim Supported
ashkenaz | sfarad | chabad | teimani | yireim

## Languages
Hebrew (he) — RTL, primary
English (en) — LTR, primary
User picks at signup. Both are equal.

## Code Style
- Functional components only, no class components
- Custom hooks for all data fetching and business logic
- Zustand stores for global state
- Explicit TypeScript types for everything — no 'any'
</file>

